@section Undo

@cindex undo
@cindex redo

Emacs keeps @strong{one linear sequence} of changes, and undo commands
are themselves recorded into it.  Redo is therefore emergent, not a
second stack.

@multitable @columnfractions .34 .66
@item @kbd{Cmd-Z}, @kbd{C-/}, @kbd{C-_} @tab Undo.
@item @kbd{Cmd-Shift-Z}, @kbd{C-?} @tab Redo (@code{undo-redo}).
@item @kbd{C-x u}, @kbd{Cmd-Shift-U} @tab vundo — the history as a
navigable tree.
@item @kbd{C-g} then @kbd{C-/} @tab The classic manoeuvre: break the undo
run, so the next undo goes @emph{forward}.
@item Select a region, then undo @tab Undoes only changes inside it.
@end multitable

The payoff is that no state is ever unreachable.  In a two-stack editor,
undoing five steps and then typing destroys those five redo states
permanently; here they are still in the sequence, so vundo can walk back
to them.

@node The Constellation
@chapter The Constellation

@cindex repositories
@cindex constellation

A directory is a HeroicLands content project when it is a git checkout
carrying a package-build configuration --- @file{package-build.config.yaml},
@file{.yml} or @file{.mjs}, any one of them.

@vindex heroiclands-project-roots
@vindex heroiclands-project-search-depth
@findex heroiclands-refresh-projects
@findex heroiclands-switch-project

@code{heroiclands-project-roots} says where to look.  Each entry is
searched like this:

@itemize
@item a directory that is @strong{itself} a project is taken as one, and is
@strong{not searched further};
@item otherwise its subdirectories are searched, to
@code{heroiclands-project-search-depth} (3).
@end itemize

Stopping at a project is the important half.  A checkout inside a
checkout --- a worktree under @file{.claude/worktrees/}, a vendored
source --- is the @emph{same} project, not another one, and descending
would find the same package twice.

@kbd{C-c h h} includes a root that is itself a git checkout and checkouts
immediately inside a directory root.  Content project discovery also
searches subdirectories to @code{heroiclands-project-search-depth}.

So a root may be a directory holding repositories, or a project itself, or
any mixture of the two:

@example
(setq heroiclands-project-roots
      '("~/work/heroiclands"      ; a directory of repositories
        "~/src/sohl-thalorna"))   ; one particular project
@end example

@strong{Left unset it searches nowhere.}  Nothing about a filesystem is
guessed --- not a conventional directory, and not the siblings of the
current checkout --- because either would be this package asserting where
your repositories ought to live.

What that costs is bounded.  The project a buffer belongs to is found by
@code{project}, not by this list, so the local index still loads and local
links still resolve and are still checked.  What needs the list is
everything @emph{across} projects: resolving a canonical
@code{<package>-<type>-<shortcode>} address into another package, and the
@kbd{C-c h} commands that span repositories.

A linked worktree counts as a checkout: there @file{.git} is a file rather
than a directory, and someone editing in one should not find the package
inert.

@kbd{M-x heroiclands-refresh-projects} searches again after a repository is
cloned or removed.  The result is cached in between, since it walks the
filesystem.

@multitable @columnfractions .18 .82
@item @kbd{C-c h h} @tab Jump to any repo.
@item @kbd{C-c h g} @tab Ripgrep across @emph{every} repo at once — the
one that finds where a thing is really defined.
@item @kbd{C-c h f} @tab Find a content note by filename, anywhere.
@item @kbd{C-c h m} @tab Find a content note by canonical key, completing
over every published address.
@item @kbd{C-c h M} @tab Reload the link manifests from disk.
@end multitable

@node Compile and Diagnostics
@chapter Compile and Diagnostics

@cindex compile
@cindex diagnostics

Project diagnostics are emitted as @code{file:line:col: severity:
message} — the GNU format @code{compilation-mode} already parses.  So
after @kbd{C-c h b}, @kbd{C-c h t} or @kbd{C-c h l}:

@multitable @columnfractions .22 .78
@item @kbd{M-g M-n} @tab Next error.
@item @kbd{M-g M-p} @tab Previous error.
@item @kbd{RET} @tab Jump to it.
@item @kbd{g} @tab Re-run.
@end multitable

@strong{The compile buffer is the authority} — it is the same check CI
runs.  Squiggles from eglot and eslint are the secondary, advisory
signal.

@node TypeScript
@chapter TypeScript

@multitable @columnfractions .22 .78
@item @kbd{C-c l a} @tab Code actions.
@item @kbd{C-c l r} @tab Rename symbol.
@item @kbd{C-c l d} @tab Documentation.
@item @kbd{C-c l i} @tab Find implementation.
@item @kbd{C-c l x} @tab @code{eslint --fix} this file.
@item @kbd{M-.} / @kbd{M-,} @tab Jump to definition / back.
@item @kbd{C-M-i} @tab Complete.
@end multitable

The server resolves TypeScript from the project's own
@file{node_modules}, so the editor and @command{tsc} are always the same
compiler.  The version-skew problem that a bundled TypeScript causes
cannot arise here.

