@node Content Language Server
@chapter Content Language Server

@findex heroiclands-server-install
@vindex heroiclands-server-directory
@vindex heroiclands-eglot-server-command

Run @kbd{M-x heroiclands-server-install} after loading
@code{heroiclands-server}.  This installs the version locked by this Emacs
package with @command{npm ci} beneath
@file{~/.emacs.d/heroiclands/content-language-server/} by default.  The
exact directory follows @code{user-emacs-directory}; customize
@code{heroiclands-server-directory} if needed.  The server package pins
its content index generator dependency to an exact version.  Project
@file{node_modules} and @file{build/} do not supply the editor runtime.
The selected server version is in @file{server/package.json};
@file{server/package-lock.json} records the installed server and generator
versions.

Load @code{heroiclands-eglot} to start the installed server in Markdown
notes under a content project's tree.  Other Markdown buffers retain
their normal Eglot setup.  @code{heroiclands-eglot-server-command} accepts
an explicit command list when developing the server.  Leave it nil for
the pinned installation.  @kbd{M-.} follows an Address or wikilink,
@kbd{C-M-.} searches indexed names and tags in the current project;
@code{all:} includes selected foreign projects.  Field prefixes such as
@code{name:}, @code{shortcode:}, and @code{type:} narrow the search.
@code{package:thalorna name:camel} selects a configured package and
searches its names.  @kbd{M-?} lists
authored references across those projects, and @kbd{M-,} returns
through Xref history.  Unsaved metadata edits do not enter search results.

@node The Content Index
@chapter The Content Index

@cindex content index
@cindex index, content

The installed content language server publishes the note tree's saved
frontmatter as JSON Lines.  Emacs reads the same private index for
completion, link following, highlighting, and jq queries.  Eglot reads
it for navigation and search.

It is one JSON object per line, in JSON Lines:

@verbatim
~/Library/Caches/HeroicLands/content-language-server/<project-hash>/metadata.jsonl
@end verbatim

That is the macOS default.  Linux uses @file{$XDG_CACHE_HOME} or
@file{~/.cache}; Windows uses the local application data directory.
The server computes the project hash and reports the exact path with
@command{heroiclands-content-language-server --print-index-path} from a
project root.  The accompanying @file{metadata.json} carries the package
identity, generator version, and checksum.  Emacs accepts complete,
compatible snapshots.  A project clean or build leaves this cache alone.

@section Rebuilding it

@kindex C-c h i
@findex heroiclands-index-rebuild

@kbd{C-c h i} (@code{heroiclands-index-rebuild}) refreshes the current
project through the pinned server and reports the path it wrote:

@verbatim
~/Library/Caches/HeroicLands/content-language-server/<project-hash>/metadata.jsonl
@end verbatim

The equivalent terminal command is
@command{heroiclands-content-language-server --rebuild-index} from the
project root.  This command refreshes only the current project.

@section What to expect of it

The index is @strong{derived and disposable}.  It lives outside the
project tree and is rebuilt from saved notes.  Nothing is authored
against it or committed.

It is also @strong{byte-stable}: rebuilding over an unchanged tree
produces an identical file.  A rebuild is never noise.

The server rebuilds the current project at startup and after saves.  Emacs
notices a complete private index publication and refreshes open buffers.
An Eglot foreign search, definition, or reference builds missing foreign
indexes on demand.  Emacs link following reads only complete indexes and
does not start foreign builds.
Use @kbd{C-c h i} when a result looks stale or a rebuild fails.

@section Navigating through Eglot

Load @code{heroiclands-eglot} alongside the other optional HeroicLands
features.  In a recognized Markdown note within a configured content
project, the mode starts the pinned content language server.

@kbd{M-.} follows the Address or wikilink at point.  @kbd{C-M-.}
searches names, aliases, ASCII spellings, shortcodes, and Addresses in
the current project; @code{tag:myth} searches tags.  Use
@code{name:camel}, @code{shortcode:bctrncml}, or @code{type:being} to
search one field.  Prefix the query with @code{all:} to include foreign
projects selected by @code{heroiclands-index-projects};
@code{all:tag:myth} searches their tags.
@code{package:thalorna name:camel} selects that package from the
configured projects and searches its names.  A package outside the
selected roots contributes no results even if its cache is present.
Results identify the owning package and open its source note.
@kbd{M-.} follows a package-qualified Address into its owning project.
@kbd{M-?} finds authored wikilinks and frontmatter Address references
in the current and selected foreign projects.  @kbd{M-,} returns to the
previous location.  Search and destinations use saved indexes, so unsaved
metadata edits do not add search results.  Reference search reads saved
Markdown source for exact body-link locations.

Set @code{heroiclands-project-roots} to the directories containing the
projects to discover.  @code{heroiclands-index-projects} accepts
@code{all}, a list of package names or paths, or @code{nil} for only the
current project.  An explicit path can select a project outside the
discovery roots.  Restart Eglot in an open content project after changing
the selection.

Loading this feature leaves ordinary Markdown with its normal Eglot
configuration.  The @kbd{C-c h .} and @kbd{C-c h ,}
commands remain available.

@node Writing Wikilinks
@chapter Writing Wikilinks

@cindex wikilinks
@cindex links, wiki

A wikilink names a note by its @emph{address} — @code{being-aurochs},
which is its type and shortcode — and optionally displays something
friendlier after a @code{|}.  The address is the half that has to be
exactly right, and the half nobody can remember.

@menu
* Completion::          Indexed names, Addresses, and anchors.
* Normalization::       What typing @code{]]} does.
* Following a Link::    Jumping to the note, or to a section.
* Seeing Them::         What the colours mean.
@end menu

@node Completion
@section Completion

@findex heroiclands-eglot-capf

In a recognized content note, Eglot asks the pinned content language server
for indexed completion candidates.  Typing @code{[[} opens completion for
notes in the current project and the foreign projects selected by
@code{heroiclands-index-projects}.  The server searches saved
@code{name.full}, aliases, shortcodes, and Addresses.  A query matches
anywhere in those fields: @code{[[camel} can offer Bactrian Camel,
Xerathian Bactrian Camel, and a note whose name merely contains
``camel''.  The candidate shows its owning package and canonical Address.

The inserted Address is the shortest spelling that still resolves to the
exact selected package, system, and target.  From Thalorna, selecting
@code{thalorna-sohl-being-bctrncml} inserts
@code{sohl-being-bctrncml} when the shorter @code{being-bctrncml} also
names a @code{none} system target.  A foreign target keeps its package
segment when that segment is needed.  This rule also applies to values
in frontmatter fields that take an Address.

After @code{#} in a wikilink, completion offers the selected note's
anchors.  The same server handles image links, with the @code{none}
system as their default.  Ordinary wikilinks default to @code{note}.
Completion stays quiet when editing a link that has already been closed.
The server reads saved metadata; save a changed note before expecting its
new name or alias in completion.

@node Normalization
@section Normalization

@vindex heroiclands-goto-canonicalize-on-close

Typing @code{]]} closes a newly entered wikilink.  When paired closing
brackets are already present, accepting a completion finishes the link and
leaves point after it.  Emacs keeps the server's exact target and adds its
display name:

@verbatim
[[sohl-being-bctrncml]] -> [[sohl-being-bctrncml|Xerathian Bactrian Camel]]
[[being-orca]]         -> [[being-orca|Killer Whale]]
@end verbatim

An exact alias stays as the display text.  An authored display half after
@code{|} stays exactly as written.  The selected target remains distinct
from another system target or another package's note, even when both
records point to the same Markdown file.

When no completion was selected, Emacs resolves a typed Address or exact
name against the available private indexes.  An unknown or ambiguous name
raises an error and asks you to choose.  A missing anchor is also reported
when its note's index is available.  If no index is available, the link is
left as typed and the buffer reports that it was not checked.

Completion and normalization apply to a link you are entering.  Editing a
closed link wakes neither.  Set
@code{heroiclands-goto-canonicalize-on-close} to @code{nil} to disable
rewriting.

@node Following a Link
@section Following a Link

@kindex C-c h .
@findex heroiclands-goto-follow

@kbd{C-c h .} on a wikilink opens the note it names.  If the link names
an anchor, it lands on that heading's line rather than at the top of the
file.  A package-qualified link opens under the owning project's content
tree when its private index is available.  The link's relative file path
is resolved against that project's configured content directory.

@kbd{C-c h ,} comes back.  It uses the same marker stack as @kbd{M-.} and
@kbd{M-,}, so the two compose.

A link naming an unknown note, or an anchor the note does not declare,
says so and lists the anchors that do exist.

@node Seeing Them
@section Seeing Them

@cindex highlighting
@cindex colours
@cindex broken links

In plain markdown every part of @code{[[address#anchor|display]]} looks
like prose.  They are coloured by part, because they are not equally
interesting:

@multitable @columnfractions .3 .7
@item @strong{address} @tab The part that has to be exactly right, and the
part nobody can read.  Given the strongest colour.
@item @strong{#anchor} @tab The section within that note, in a lighter
shade of the same.
@item @strong{|display} @tab The words a reader actually sees.  Kept close
to body text on purpose --- it @emph{is} the prose, and colouring it like a
link would make the sentence harder to read rather than easier.
@item @strong{brackets} @tab Structure, so they recede.
@end multitable

@subsection Validation through Flymake

@cindex Flymake
@cindex diagnostics

Eglot receives reference diagnostics from the pinned content language
server and shows them through Flymake.  An invalid Address, anchor, image
embed, or declared frontmatter Address has one finding at its source
position.  @kbd{M-x flymake-show-buffer-diagnostics} lists the findings.
The server updates them after edits and saves, so an old finding disappears
when its source becomes valid.

The colour of a wikilink describes its syntax only.  The language server
owns correctness, using saved indexes and the open buffer text.  A build
also checks the published output; it may report findings outside the open
buffer or under a different publication configuration.

@subsection A package name is not a directory name

@findex heroiclands-project-package

A wikilink names a @emph{package}; a root holds @emph{directories}, and in
this constellation the two have never once matched --- 
@file{Song-of-Heroic-Lands-FoundryVTT} publishes @code{sohl},
@file{sohl-thalorna} publishes @code{thalorna}, @file{HarnMaster-3-FoundryVTT}
publishes @code{hm3}.

So @code{heroiclands-index-projects} matches an entry against the package
name first and the directory name second: write @code{thalorna}, the name
already known from links, and it finds the project wherever it is cloned.

The name is read from @code{contentPackage} in the configuration --- the one
fact worth taking from that file rather than from an index, since it is
wanted @emph{before} an index exists.  Both forms are read, with a
difference that matters: in YAML a bare word is a string, but in JavaScript
it is a variable, so only a quoted literal is taken from a @file{.mjs}.  A
computed one yields nothing rather than an invented answer.

@subsection Not held is not the same as not there

@vindex heroiclands-index-projects

A wikilink may name a note in another package by its canonical
@code{<package>-<system>-<type>-<shortcode>} Address ---
@code{sohl-note-being-aurochs}
cited from a @code{thalorna} note.  Resolving one means holding that
package's index too, so @code{heroiclands-index-projects} says which to
read; it defaults to every project found under
@code{heroiclands-project-roots}.  The language server builds a selected
foreign project's index when Eglot completion or navigation needs it.
Emacs link following uses its index when a complete one exists.

A foreign package whose index is unavailable is reported as unavailable
by Flymake.  The server does not claim that its targets are broken without
an index to check.  Syntax colouring remains available without Eglot or an
index; the lighter reads @samp{HL?} until the local index is ready.
@xref{The Mode}.

@node Live Preview
@chapter Live Preview

@cindex preview, browser
@cindex tables, SQL
@findex heroiclands-preview-toggle
@findex heroiclands-preview-refresh

@kbd{C-c h p} opens the current HeroicLands note in a browser window. The
preview shows the page body with the site's CSS. SQL tables, wikilinks,
images, code fences, and GM disclosures use the site's transformations.
Links and heading anchors are visible but do not navigate. A note with a
generated infobox shows its boxes in the site's order. On a wide screen the
rail sits beside the prose; on a narrow screen it sits above it.

The current buffer is sent to the renderer three seconds after typing stops.
Typing again resets the timer. Unsaved edits can be previewed without
writing the note or the site's build directory. Only a completed render
replaces the page; an error appears above the last good page and in Emacs.
The browser keeps an approximate scroll position when a new render arrives.
Each note has its own preview; @kbd{C-c h p} again stops that note's renderer.
Closing a note buffer stops its renderer as well.

@kbd{C-c h P} reloads saved project notes, configuration, and the theme CSS.
Use it after another note or an asset changes. The preview reads the same
project configuration names as @code{heroiclands-mode}: @file{.yaml},
@file{.yml}, and @file{.mjs}.

@vindex heroiclands-preview-idle-delay
@vindex heroiclands-preview-new-window
@vindex heroiclands-preview-show-infobox
@vindex heroiclands-preview-node

The renderer uses the pinned package-build installation from
@code{heroiclands-server-directory}, the project's installed
@code{@@heroiclands/hugo-theme}, Node, and Pandoc. Run
@kbd{M-x heroiclands-server-install} if the pinned installation is absent.
Set @code{heroiclands-preview-idle-delay} to adjust the pause. The default
@code{heroiclands-preview-new-window} asks @code{browse-url} for a separate
browser window; the browser decides whether that request opens a window or
tab. Set @code{heroiclands-preview-show-infobox} to @code{nil} in a buffer
to show only its body, then run @kbd{C-c h P}. The infobox reads generated
page metadata, so an unsaved change to its inputs appears after the same
idle pause as the body. @code{heroiclands-preview-node} selects the Node
executable.

@kbd{C-c C-c p} remains Markdown mode's one-time preview command.

@node Querying Content
@chapter Querying Content

@kindex C-c h I
@findex heroiclands-index-query

@kbd{C-c h I} runs a jq filter against the index and shows the results in
a buffer.  Each record is a note's own frontmatter, so a query reads
exactly what the note writes.

@section Recipes

Beings in a category:

@verbatim
select(.type == "being" and .sohl.kbcat == "animal") | .shortcode
@end verbatim

Notes missing something they should have:

@verbatim
select(.type == "being" and (.sohl.kbcat | not)) | .file.path
@end verbatim

What a stale query would have matched — the check that would have caught
eight dead Bestiary tables:

@verbatim
select(.type == "creature") | .file.path
@end verbatim

Every anchor a note declares, with its line:

@verbatim
select(.address.slug == "doc-crafting")
  | .anchors[] | "\(.line)  \(.slug)"
@end verbatim

From a terminal, the same thing:

@verbatim
jq -r 'select(.type == "being") | .name.full' \
  ~/Library/Caches/HeroicLands/content-language-server/<project-hash>/metadata.jsonl
@end verbatim

@node Record Format
@chapter Record Format

Each line is a note's whole frontmatter, unflattened — you address
@code{sohl.body.weight.base} because that is what the note says — plus
these derived fields.  A note that tries to author one of them is a build
error rather than a silent overwrite.

@table @code
@item package
The package the tree compiles as.

@item file
Where the note sits: @code{path}, @code{folder}, @code{name}.  The path
is relative to the content root, never absolute.

@item address
@code{slug} is what goes inside @code{[[@dots{}]]} within the package
(@code{being-aurochs}); @code{canonical} includes package and system
(@code{sohl-note-being-aurochs}).  Both @code{null} for a note with no type or
shortcode, which has no address at all.

@item anchors
Every @code{@{#slug@}} heading the body declares, each with its
@code{name}, @code{level}, @code{line} in the file, and the
@code{slug#anchor} @code{link} that reaches it.

@item nameAscii
@code{name.full} reduced to printable 7-bit ASCII.  Transliterated, not
stripped: @code{Kûrbúl ¾-Helm} becomes @code{Kurbul 3/4-Helm},
@code{Ærling} becomes @code{AErling}, @code{Þorn} becomes @code{Thorn}.

@item aliasesAscii
The same for @code{name.aliases}, in the authored order.  An empty array
when a note has none — never @code{null}.
@end table

@node Handlebars Helpers
@chapter Handlebars Helpers

@cindex handlebars
@cindex templates

Inside a @file{.hbs} template, typing @code{@{@{} completes across 47
helpers, annotated with their source and with real documentation pulled
from the definitions:

@itemize
@item 15 @strong{sohl}, from @file{src/utils/handlebars-helpers.ts} and
@file{src/sohl.ts}
@item 26 @strong{foundry}, from Foundry's @code{registerHelper} block,
with @code{[DEPRECATED]} flagged
@item 6 @strong{builtin} — Handlebars' own keywords
@end itemize

@code{@{@{#} and @code{@{@{/} put the block helpers (@code{each},
@code{if}, @code{unless}, @code{with}) first.

@multitable @columnfractions .18 .82
@item @kbd{C-c h H} @tab Describe a helper.
@item @kbd{C-c h R} @tab Rescan the sources and rebuild the table.
@end multitable

Names are @strong{read from the source}, never restated — add a helper to
@file{src/}, press @kbd{C-c h R}, and it appears.  Foundry's module lives
on an external volume; if it is unmounted the cached Foundry entries are
kept.

@node Special Characters
@chapter Special Characters

@cindex special characters
@cindex accents
@cindex em dash

@file{assets/content} holds 5,916 non-ASCII characters, 4,498 of them em
dashes, so typing them has to be easy.

@section macOS modifier keys

@multitable @columnfractions .28 .72
@item @strong{Left} Option @tab @code{Meta} — @kbd{M-x}, @kbd{M-:},
@kbd{M-w}.
@item @strong{Right} Option @tab Itself: the macOS character layer.
@item Cmd @tab @code{Super} — @kbd{s-z} undo, @kbd{s-=} bigger font.
@item @key{ESC} @tab A sticky Meta: @key{ESC} then @kbd{x} is @kbd{M-x}.
Always works.
@end multitable

Right Option stays a normal Option @strong{deliberately}: with both
Options bound to Meta, none of those 5,916 characters could be typed.

@section Bindings that work on any keyboard

@multitable @columnfractions .2 .3 .2 .3
@item @kbd{C-c u -} @tab — em dash @tab @kbd{C-c u .} @tab … ellipsis
@item @kbd{C-c u n} @tab – en dash @tab @kbd{C-c u x} @tab × multiply
@item @kbd{C-c u '} @tab ’ right quote @tab @kbd{C-c u m} @tab − minus
@item @kbd{C-c u "} @tab ” right dquote @tab @kbd{C-c u l} @tab £ pound
@item @kbd{C-c u d} @tab ° degree @tab @kbd{C-c u >} @tab → arrow
@item @kbd{C-c u s} @tab § section @tab @tab
@end multitable

@kbd{C-c u @key{RET}} inserts anything else by name or hex — @kbd{2014
@key{RET}}, or @kbd{EM DASH @key{RET}}.  @kbd{C-x 8 @key{RET}} is the
same command.

@section Accented letters

The corner key labelled @code{ctrl/opt} on the MX Keys S @emph{is} right
Option, so the standard dead-key sequences work for most accents.

@multitable @columnfractions .2 .45 .35
@headitem Want @tab Right-Option sequence @tab In content
@item á Á é í ú ý @tab @kbd{Opt-e} then the letter @tab 177
@item û â î @tab @kbd{Opt-i} then the letter @tab 78
@item ü ë @tab @kbd{Opt-u} then the letter @tab 20
@item è @tab @kbd{Opt-`} then the letter @tab 10
@item ç @tab @kbd{Opt-c} @tab 2
@end multitable

@strong{Macron is the exception} — macOS has no Option sequence for it at
all, and this content uses 23.  So it is bound directly:

@multitable @columnfractions .2 .3 .2 .3
@item @kbd{C-c u = a} @tab ā @tab @kbd{C-c u = o} @tab ō
@item @kbd{C-c u = i} @tab ī @tab @kbd{C-c u = u} @tab ū
@item @kbd{C-c u = A} @tab Ā @tab @kbd{C-c u = e} @tab ē
@end multitable

Also @kbd{C-c u A} gives Æ and @kbd{C-c u a} gives æ.  Anything else:
@kbd{C-c u @key{RET}}.

@node Housekeeping
@chapter Housekeeping

@section Format on save

Prettier runs on save — asynchronously, so it never blocks — for
@file{ts}, @file{tsx}, @file{js}, @file{mjs}, @file{json}, @file{css},
@file{markdown}, @file{yaml}, @file{hbs} and @file{html}, using the
project's own Prettier and configuration.

@section Occasional rebuilds

@multitable @columnfractions .45 .55
@item @kbd{C-c h i} @tab Content index, after adding notes.
@item @kbd{C-c h R} @tab Handlebars helper table, after adding a helper.
@item @kbd{C-c h M} @tab Link manifests, after they change on disk.
@item @code{M-x my/install-grammars} @tab Reinstall tree-sitter grammars.
@end multitable

Font and size knobs are the two @code{defvar}s at the top of
@file{init.el}.

@section Rebuilding this manual

@verbatim
makeinfo -o ~/.config/emacs/info/heroiclands.info \
  ~/.config/emacs/doc/heroiclands.texi
@end verbatim

@node Troubleshooting
@chapter Troubleshooting

@table @asis
@item A note I just added is not offered
Save the note and let the server finish indexing.  Use @kbd{C-c h i}
to refresh the current project on demand.

@item Completion does not open in a link
It only arms when you @emph{type} @code{[[}.  Editing an existing link
deliberately does not wake it.  Retype the link, or use @kbd{C-c h .} to
navigate instead.

@item ``No content index built''
Run @kbd{M-x heroiclands-server-install} if the server is absent, then
start Eglot in a content note or run @kbd{C-c h i} in this project.

@item Following a link says the note is unknown
Either the index is stale (@kbd{C-c h i}) or the address really is wrong.
Completion tells you which, by whether it can offer it.

@item An anchor I added is not found
Anchors come from the index too.  Rebuild.

@item Everything is silent in this buffer
The content machinery runs in a Markdown file whose opening YAML
frontmatter declares both @code{type} and @code{shortcode}.  Check the
@samp{HL} lighter; if it is absent, @kbd{M-x heroiclands-mode} turns it
on explicitly.  Eglot also needs a configured content project and an
installed server.

@item A helper I just wrote does not complete
@kbd{C-c h R} rescans the sources.

@item Emacs feels cold — no manifests, no language server
You are probably in a rival instance.  Quit it and attach to the daemon
with @strong{Emacs Client}.  @xref{Starting Emacs}.
@end table

